title: The duplicate hash cache
tags: Duplicates
aliases: hash
kind: reference
summary: The scan keeps its hashes, stamped with the file's mtime and size, and trusts them only while both still match.

Hashing is the expensive part of a scan, so the hashes are stored on the file's
metarecord and reused: `mfr_partial_hash` (the first and last 4 KiB) and
`mfr_full_hash` (the whole file), both xxHash3-64 — the fingerprints the watcher
and reconcile use to follow files around.

A stored hash is only usable if the file has not changed since, and the watcher
does not see every change: a subtree whose watching is off is frozen on purpose,
and a file modified there keeps a hash that no longer describes it. So the scan
records, together with the hashes, the state they were computed from:

* `mfr_hash_mtime` — the file's modification time at hash time;
* `mfr_hash_size` — its size at hash time.

A stored hash is trusted only when ''both'' still match the live `stat`;
otherwise it is recomputed. Both are needed: the modification time alone misses a
rewrite that preserves it, and `mfr_size` cannot serve because a reconcile
updates it without touching the hashes. The watcher, when it sees a file's
content change, clears the hashes, their stamp and the file's group link.

This does not contradict the rule that the modification time never decides
identity. Here it decides that a derived value is ''stale'', and it fails safe: a
mismatch costs a recomputation, never a wrong match.

The stamp is a strong heuristic, not a proof: modification times have one-second
granularity on some filesystems and can be set backwards (`touch -t`,
`rsync --times`, an archive extraction), so a same-second, same-size rewrite
slips through. `mf duplicate scan --rehash` ignores every stored hash.

`mf duplicate clear` leaves the cache alone: it is valid, expensive to rebuild,
and useful to reconcile and sync on its own.
